ant me to write a quick script right now that draws an animated stickman waving, just so you can see how it works?","thinking":"**Considering Open Source Animation**\n\nI'm currently considering open-source options for animation. No specific tools are needed for this initial phase, so I'm focusing on identifying potential open-source resources. The goal is to determine which options are suitable for various animation needs, even for personal projects.\n\n\n**Assessing AI Animation Control**\n\nI'm now assessing my ability to directly animate characters with the identified tools. My strengths lie in code-based animation: I'm proficient with Motion Canvas and Remotion, able to generate dynamic 2D visuals and UI elements via TypeScript/React. While I can handle simple JSON manipulation for Excalidraw, it's not ideal for drawing complex characters. I can use the Blender Python API, `bpy`, to build primitive shapes and apply materials. I can't yet directly translate natural language descriptions to organic character movement within any of these tools.\n\n\n**Clarifying Animation Constraints**\n\nMy focus has shifted to clarifying my capabilities and limitations within the scope of animation. I can manipulate a rigged 3D character by coding in Blender using Python if they are already loaded, and generate 2D stickman animations via p5.js with trigonometric functions. I will inform the user I don't have hands to manipulate GUIs, so tools like Krita, Synfig, and Excalidraw are unsuitable.\n\n\n"}
