{"step_index":134,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T19:39:34Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-01T01:09:34+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-01T01:09:34+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/find-skills/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 204\nTotal Bytes: 7106\nShowing lines 1 to 204\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: find-skills\n3: description: Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like \"how do I do X\", \"find a skill for X\", \"is there a skill that can...\", or express interest in extending capabilities. This skill should be used when the user is looking for functionality that might exist as an installable skill.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Find Skills (Windows Compatible Version)\n7: \n8: This skill helps you discover and install skills from the open agent skills ecosystem.\n9: \n10: **This is a Windows-compatible fork that fixes the empty output issue in Claude Code on Windows.**\n11: \n12: ## ⚠️ CRITICAL: Windows Compatibility\n13: \n14: **On Windows, you MUST use PowerShell to run skills commands!**\n15: \n16: The default Bash/Git Bash environment on Windows does NOT work with `npx skills` - commands will return empty output.\n17: \n18: **Always use this format on Windows:**\n19: ```bash\n20: powershell -Command \"npx skills find '[query]'\"\n21: powershell -Command \"npx skills add [package] -g -y\"\n22: powershell -Command \"npx skills list -g\"\n23: ```\n24: \n25: **Example:**\n26: ```bash\n27: # ❌ WRONG - will return empty on Windows\n28: npx skills find \"react\"\n29: \n30: # ✅ CORRECT - works on Windows\n31: powershell -Command \"npx skills find 'react'\"\n32: ```\n33: \n34: ## When to Use This Skill\n35: \n36: Use this skill when the user:\n37: \n38: - Asks \"how do I do X\" where X might be a common task with an existing skill\n39: - Says \"find a skill for X\" or \"is there a skill for X\"\n40: - Asks \"can you do X\" where X is a specialized capability\n41: - Expresses interest in extending agent capabilities\n42: - Wants to search for tools, templates, or workflows\n43: - Mentions they wish they had help with a specific domain (design, testing, deployment, etc.)\n44: \n45: ## What is the Skills CLI?\n46: \n47: The Skills CLI (`npx skills`) is the package manager for the open agent skills ecosystem. Skills are modular packages that extend agent capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, and tools.\n48: \n49: **Key commands (Windows format):**\n50: \n51: - `powershell -Command \"npx skills find '[query]'\"` - Search for skills\n52: - `powershell -Command \"npx skills add [package] -g -y\"` - Install a skill\n53: - `powershell -Command \"npx skills list -g\"` - List installed skills\n54: - `powershell -Command \"npx skills check\"` - Check for skill updates\n55: - `powershell -Command \"npx skills update\"` - Update all installed skills\n56: \n57: **Browse skills at:** https://skills.sh/\n58: \n59: ## How to Help Users Find Skills\n60: \n61: ### Step 1: Understand What They Need\n62: \n63: When a user asks for help with something, identify:\n64: \n65: 1. The domain (e.g., React, testing, design, deployment)\n66: 2. The specific task (e.g., writing tests, creating animations, reviewing PRs)\n67: 3. Whether this is a common enough task that a skill likely exists\n68: \n69: ### Step 2: Search for Skills\n70: \n71: Run the find command with a relevant query.\n72: \n73: **On Windows (REQUIRED):**\n74: ```bash\n75: powershell -Command \"npx skills find '[query]'\"\n76: ```\n77: \n78: **On macOS/Linux:**\n79: ```bash\n80: npx skills find [query]\n81: ```\n82: \n83: For example (Windows format):\n84: \n85: - User asks \"how do I make my React app faster?\" → `powershell -Command \"npx skills find 'react performance'\"`\n86: - User asks \"can you help me with PR reviews?\" → `powershell -Command \"npx skills find 'pr review'\"`\n87: - User asks \"I need to create a changelog\" → `powershell -Command \"npx skills find 'changelog'\"`\n88: - User asks \"数据分析\" (data analysis in Chinese) → `powershell -Command \"npx skills find 'data analysis'\"` (Note: Search only supports English keywords!)\n89: \n90: The command will return results like:\n91: \n92: ```\n93: Install with npx skills add <owner/repo@skill>\n94: \n95: vercel-labs/agent-skills@vercel-react-best-practices\n96: └ https://skills.sh/vercel-labs/agent-skills/vercel-react-best-practices\n97: ```\n98: \n99: ### Step 3: Present Options to the User\n100: \n101: When you find relevant skills, present them to the user with:\n102: \n103: 1. The skill name and what it does\n104: 2. The install command they can run (Windows format!)\n105: 3. A link to learn more at skills.sh\n106: \n107: Example response:\n108: \n109: ```\n110: I found a skill that might help! The \"vercel-react-best-practices\" skill provides\n111: React and Next.js performance optimization guidelines from Vercel Engineering.\n112: \n113: To install it (Windows):\n114: powershell -Command \"npx skills add vercel-labs/agent-skills@vercel-react-best-practices -g -y\"\n115: \n116: Learn more: https://skills.sh/vercel-labs/agent-skills/vercel-react-best-practices\n117: ```\n118: \n119: ### Step 4: Offer to Install\n120: \n121: If the user wants to proceed, you can install the skill for them.\n122: \n123: **On Windows (REQUIRED):**\n124: ```bash\n125: powershell -Command \"npx skills add <owner/repo@skill> -g -y\"\n126: ```\n127: \n128: **On macOS/Linux:**\n129: ```bash\n130: npx skills add <owner/repo@skill> -g -y\n131: ```\n132: \n133: The `-g` flag installs globally (user-level) and `-y` skips confirmation prompts.\n134: \n135: ## Common Skill Categories\n136: \n137: When searching, consider these common categories:\n138: \n139: | Category        | Example Queries                          |\n140: | --------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n141: | Web Development | react, nextjs, typescript, css, tailwind |\n142: | Testing         | testing, jest, playwright, e2e           |\n143: | DevOps          | deploy, docker, kubernetes, ci-cd        |\n144: | Documentation   | docs, readme, changelog, api-docs        |\n145: | Code Quality    | review, lint, refactor, best-practices   |\n146: | Design          | ui, ux, design-system, accessibility     |\n147: | Productivity    | workflow, automation, git                |\n148: | Data Analysis   | data analysis, pandas, jupyter           |\n149: \n150: ## Chinese to English Keyword Reference\n151: \n152: **Important: Search only supports English keywords!**\n153: \n154: | Chinese (中文) | English Keywords |\n155: | ------------- | ---------------- |\n156: | 数据分析 | data analysis |\n157: | 做PPT | ppt, presentation |\n158: | 写文章 | writing |\n159: | 代码审查 | code review |\n160: | 部署上线 | deploy, deployment |\n161: | 写测试 | testing |\n162: | 做视频 | video, remotion |\n163: \n164: ## Tips for Effective Searches\n165: \n166: 1. **Use specific keywords**: \"react testing\" is better than just \"testing\"\n167: 2. **Try alternative terms**: If \"deploy\" doesn't work, try \"deployment\" or \"ci-cd\"\n168: 3. **Check popular sources**: Many skills come from `vercel-labs/agent-skills` or `ComposioHQ/awesome-claude-skills`\n169: 4. **Use English keywords**: Chinese search will return empty results\n170: \n171: ## When No Skills Are Found\n172: \n173: If no relevant skills exist:\n174: \n175: 1. Acknowledge that no existing skill was found\n176: 2. Offer to help with the task directly using your general capabilities\n177: 3. Suggest the user could create their own skill with `npx skills init`\n178: \n179: Example:\n180: \n181: ```\n182: I searched for skills related to \"xyz\" but didn't find any matches.\n183: I can still help you with this task directly! Would you like me to proceed?\n184: \n185: If this is something you do often, you could create your own skill:\n186: powershell -Command \"npx skills init my-xyz-skill\"\n187: ```\n188: \n189: ## Troubleshooting\n190: \n191: ### Q: Search returns empty on Windows?\n192: A: Make sure you're using `powershell -Command \"npx skills find '...'\"` format, not direct `npx skills find`.\n193: \n194: ### Q: Chinese search returns nothing?\n195: A: Search only supports English keywords. Translate your query to English first.\n196: \n197: ### Q: How to verify installation?\n198: A: Run `powershell -Command \"npx skills list -g\"` to see all installed skills.\n199: \n200: ---\n201: \n202: **Original skill by Vercel Labs: https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills**\n203: **Windows fix by: https://github.com/KimYx0207/findskill**\n204: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
