{"step_index":148,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T19:44:06Z","thinking":"**Considering Tools Selection**\n\nI've zeroed in on the `write_to_file` tool to craft the Python script. Specifically, I intend to use it to create the Python code required. The primary focus now is on utilizing this tool effectively, given the defined path to Blender.\n\n\n**Constructing the Python Script**\n\nI've crafted the core Python script now, with object clearance, Suzanne creation, material addition, and rotation animation included. I've set up keyframes and linear interpolation for smoother animation, aiming to get this Blender animation functional. The path is set to the file provided.\n\n\n**Adding Final Touches**\n\nI've integrated the camera and light setup into the script now. I've also configured the render settings, including resolution, frame range, and crucially, the output format as MP4 using the H264 codec. I'm finalizing the script's output path for the rendered animation. The final step is to execute the render command.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"import bpy\nimport math\nimport os\n\n# 1. Clear everything in the default scene (cube, etc)\nbpy.ops.object.select_all(action='SELECT')\nbpy.ops.object.delete(use_global=False)\n\n# 2. Add Suzanne (The famous Blender Monkey Head)\nbpy.ops.mesh.primitive_monkey_add(size=2, location=(0, 0, 0))\nmonkey = bpy.context.active_object\n\n# Give it a shiny red material\nmat = bpy.data.materials.new(name=\"MonkeyMaterial\")\nmat.use_nodes = True\nbsdf = mat.node_tree.nodes[\"Principled BSDF\"]\nbsdf.inputs['Base Color'].default_value = (0.8, 0.1, 0.1, 1) # Red\nmonkey.data.materials.append(mat)\nbpy.ops.object.shade_smooth() # Make the monkey look smooth\n\n# 3. Animate the Monkey spinning 360 degrees\nmonkey.rotation_mode = 'XYZ'\nmonkey.rotation_euler = (0, 0, 0)\nmonkey.keyframe_insert(data_path=\"rotation_euler\", frame=1)\n\nmonkey.rotation_euler = (0, 0, math.radians(360))\nmonkey.keyframe_insert(data_path=\"rotation_euler\", frame=60)\n\n# Make the spinning smooth and continuous\nfor fcurve in monkey.animation_data.action.fcurves:\n    for kf in fcurve.keyframe_points:\n        kf.interpolation = 'LINEAR'\n\n# 4. Add a Camera to film it\nbpy.ops.object.camera_add(location=(0, -6, 1), rotation=(math.radians(80), 0, 0))\ncam = bpy.context.active_object\nbpy.context.scene.camera = cam\n\n# 5. Add a Light so we can see\nbpy.ops.object.light_add(type='SUN', location=(5, -5, 5))\nlight = bpy.context.active_object\nlight.data.energy = 5.0\n\n# 6. Render Settings\nscene = bpy.context.scene\nscene.frame_start = 1\nscene.frame_end = 60 # 60 frames = 2.5 seconds at 24fps\nscene.render.resolution_x = 800\nscene.render.resolution_y = 600\n\n# Save as MP4\noutput_path = os.path.join(os.path.abspath(os.path.dirname(__file__)), \"spinning_monkey.mp4\")\nscene.render.filepath = output_path\nscene.render.image_settings.file_format = 'FFMPEG'\nscene.render.ffmpeg.format = 'MPEG4'\nscene.render.ffmpeg.codec = 'H264'\n\n# 7. RENDER THE VIDEO\nprint(f\"Rendering to: {output_path}\")\nbpy.ops.render.render(animation=True)\n","Description":"Writing Blender headless animation script","Overwrite":false,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\college\\College sem 5\\blender_animate.py","toolAction":"Writing Blender script","toolSummary":"Write blender script"}}]}
