{"step_index":165,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T19:48:15Z","thinking":"**Initiating Physics Simulation**\n\nI've begun setting up the environment. I've moved the required files and decided on a classic physics simulation, specifically rigid body dynamics. My next step will be creating a Python script within my Blender directory to get things started.\n\n\n**Building the Blender Script**\n\nI'm now deep in the process of scripting the Blender simulation, focusing on a tower of cubes demolition scene. I've cleared the default scene and added the ground plane. The Python code now includes creating the ground with a passive rigid body and a dark material, and I have set the collision shape as a box. I am adding a cube and applying physics to it.\n\n\n**Adding Cubes and a Ball**\n\nI've got the cube tower generation fully operational; I'm populating a 3x3x5 array of cubes using nested loops and assigning a uniform material. Each cube gets physics applied, with set mass and friction. Now, I'm constructing a wrecking ball; it's a UV sphere, also with physics, and I'm adding material and shading to the ball.\n\n\n**Animating the Wrecking Ball**\n\nI've set up the wrecking ball's initial position and keyframed its impact. Now I'm preparing for the collision. The ball will start kinematic, then become dynamic after the impact. I've programmed a heavy mass value to ensure sufficient destructive power in the scene. I am using animation to control its motion for the first 25 frames. I am setting the scene's end frame to 80, after the impact.\n\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"import bpy\nimport os\nimport math\n\n# 1. Clear scene\nbpy.ops.object.select_all(action='SELECT')\nbpy.ops.object.delete(use_global=False)\n\n# Make sure rigid body world exists\nbpy.ops.rigidbody.world_add()\n\n# 2. Add Ground (Passive Rigid Body)\nbpy.ops.mesh.primitive_plane_add(size=20, location=(0, 0, 0))\nground = bpy.context.active_object\nbpy.ops.rigidbody.object_add()\nground.rigid_body.type = 'PASSIVE'\nground.rigid_body.collision_shape = 'BOX'\n# Ground material\nmat_g = bpy.data.materials.new(\"Ground\")\nmat_g.use_nodes = True\nmat_g.node_tree.nodes[\"Principled BSDF\"].inputs['Base Color'].default_value = (0.1, 0.1, 0.1, 1)\nground.data.materials.append(mat_g)\n\n# 3. Add Tower of Cubes (Active Rigid Bodies)\nmat_cube = bpy.data.materials.new(\"Cube\")\nmat_cube.use_nodes = True\nmat_cube.node_tree.nodes[\"Principled BSDF\"].inputs['Base Color'].default_value = (0.1, 0.5, 0.8, 1)\n\nfor z in range(5):\n    for x in range(3):\n        for y in range(3):\n            bpy.ops.mesh.primitive_cube_add(size=0.8, location=(x*0.85 - 0.85, y*0.85 - 0.85, z*0.85 + 0.4))\n            cube = bpy.context.active_object\n            cube.data.materials.append(mat_cube)\n            bpy.ops.rigidbody.object_add()\n            cube.rigid_body.mass = 1.0\n            cube.rigid_body.friction = 0.5\n\n# 4. Add Wrecking Ball (Active Rigid Body with animation)\nbpy.ops.mesh.primitive_uv_sphere_add(radius=1.5, location=(-8, 0, 2))\nball = bpy.context.active_object\nbpy.ops.object.shade_smooth()\nmat_ball = bpy.data.materials.new(\"Ball\")\nmat_ball.use_nodes = True\nmat_ball.node_tree.nodes[\"Principled BSDF\"].inputs['Base Color'].default_value = (0.8, 0.5, 0.1, 1)\nmat_ball.node_tree.nodes[\"Principled BSDF\"].inputs['Metallic'].default_value = 1.0\nball.data.materials.append(mat_ball)\n\nbpy.ops.rigidbody.object_add()\nball.rigid_body.mass = 500.0  # Very heavy\nball.rigid_body.kinematic = True # Let animation control it initially\n\n# Animate the wrecking ball smashing into the tower\nball.keyframe_insert(data_path=\"location\", frame=1)\nball.location = (5, 0, 2)\nball.keyframe_insert(data_path=\"location\", frame=20)\n\n# Turn off kinematic so physics takes over after impact\nball.keyframe_insert(data_path=\"rigid_body.kinematic\", frame=20)\nball.rigid_body.kinematic = False\nball.keyframe_insert(data_path=\"rigid_body.kinematic\", frame=21)\n\n# 5. Add Camera and Light\nbpy.ops.object.camera_add(location=(8, -10, 6), rotation=(math.radians(60), 0, math.radians(45)))\ncam = bpy.context.active_object\nbpy.context.scene.camera = cam\n\nbpy.ops.object.light_add(type='SUN', location=(5, -5, 10))\nlight = bpy.context.active_object\nlight.data.energy = 5.0\n\n# 6. Render Settings\nscene = bpy.context.scene\nscene.frame_start = 1\nscene.frame_end = 80  # Render 80 frames\nscene.render.resolution_x = 800\nscene.render.resolution_y = 600\n\n# Bake Physics (important for headless rendering)\nbpy.ops.ptcache.bake_all(bake=True)\n\n# Save as PNG sequence\noutput_folder = os.path.join(os.path.abspath(os.path.dirname(__file__)), \"smash_render\")\nif not os.path.exists(output_folder):\n    os.makedirs(output_folder)\nscene.render.filepath = os.path.join(output_folder, \"frame_\")\nscene.render.image_settings.file_format = 'PNG'\n\n# 7. Render\nprint(f\"Rendering physics simulation to: {output_folder}\")\nbpy.ops.render.render(animation=True)\n","Description":"Creating physics wrecking ball animation in Blender","Overwrite":false,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\blender\\physics_smash.py","toolAction":"Writing wrecking ball blender script","toolSummary":"Physics simulation script"}}]}
